<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Schaumburg-Lippe</h1><p class="st-meta">1647 CE – 1918 CE · capital Bückeburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny north German state at Bückeburg that survived from the Thirty Years' War to 1918, famed for a soldier-count's military academy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the counts of Schaumburg died out in 1640, their lands were divided in 1647. Philip, a younger brother of the Count of Lippe, received a share including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buckeburg">Bückeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stadthagen">Stadthagen</a>, founding Schaumburg-Lippe, while the rest went to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Hesse-Kassel</a> and Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-count-of-schaumburg-lippe">Count William</a>, in the 18th century, was a military reformer who built the island fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmstein">Wilhelmstein</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steinhuder-meer">Steinhuder Meer</a> and ran a school for officers where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a> trained. He also commanded allied forces in Portugal in 1762. The princely title came in 1807. The economy relied on farming and sandstone quarries, and the princes became wealthy landowners.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Schaumburg-Lippe joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a> and in 1871 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> as one of its smallest states. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-ii-prince-of-schaumburg-lippe">Adolf II</a> abdicated in November 1918, and it continued as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-schaumburg-lippe">Free State of Schaumburg-Lippe</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe.jpg" alt="Principality of Schaumburg-Lippe" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_F%C3%BCrstentum_Schaumburg-Lippe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1647 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bückeburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip I, William, Adolf II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schaumburg-Lippe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=principality-of-schaumburg-lippe" aria-label="Principality of Schaumburg-Lippe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4883 373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Schaumburg-Lippe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5203 629L5206 630M5203 629L5229 694M5203 629L5199 691M5203 629L5300 622M5203 629L5203 629"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5206 630h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5203 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5203 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5203 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bückeburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
